id: rule-seq-no-r
title: No possible R1
topic: Sequential composition
example-of: SEQ
derived: false

task:
  Use the rule SEQ to try to derive this triple. The intermediate
  assertion $R1$ is computed backwards from $Q$.

pre: x = 0
post: x = 3

scheme: SEQ
  First premise: {«P»} x := x + 1 {«R1»}
  Second premise: {«R1»} x := x * 2 {«Q»}
  ---
  {«P»} x := x + 1; x := x * 2 {«Q»}

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  x := x + 1;    // i1
  {R1}
  x := x * 2     // i2
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of x := x * 2
part: Second premise
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i2
text:
  We substitute the variable $x$ with the assignment expression $x * 2$ in
  $Q$.
calc:
  wp(x := x * 2, Q)
  = (x = 3)[x <- x * 2]     // Q is x = 3
  = x * 2 = 3               // x replaced by x * 2
  = false                   // x * 2 is even and 3 is odd
set: R1 = false

step: R2, the weakest precondition of x := x + 1
part: First premise
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the variable $x$ with $x + 1$ in $R1$. The formula
  $false$ contains no $x$.
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, R1)
  = (false)[x <- x + 1]     // R1 is false
  = false                   // false contains no x
set: R2 = false

step: P does not imply R2
part: First premise
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R2
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «R2»
vc: x = 0 ==> false
status: fails
counterexample: x = 0
why:
  From $x = 0$ the program computes $x = 1$ and then $x = 2$, so $Q$ is
  false at the end. The triple is not valid.
